PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.4/10 (bar 8), 1 level(s) below 6/10: This is a well-engineered, fully line-solvable set, and the chapter structure (one mechanic per island) is clear. Difficulty targets are mostly met: the last-third casual average is about 62%, the skilled rate stays in range, and the easy-streak rule holds. Chapter finales do their job, and the best boards are 4, 18, 23, 24, 36, 37, 48, 60, 72, 96, 108 and 120. Weaknesses:

- **Repetition.** The same trick, "one row/column fills exactly, then chain", is the opening note in about half the levels. The exact-fit colour rows in L25-L36 and the "read the hidden line from its crossings" smudge levels in L73-L84 sound alike. Several breathers (L7, L19, L31, L43) reuse an earlier fill-notch board instead of a new shape idea.
- **Chapters with weak ideas.** Stamp is the thinnest. L51 and L52 repeat L50, and L70's stamp is nearly decorative. Observatory's "deep-chain" is a label rather than a distinct mechanic, so L97 teaches nothing new.
- **Data fault.** L82 is labelled "smudged row 8" but its text says row 7. Fix this before launch.
- **Pacing.** L99 is billed as gentle but plays at 63% casual between two harder boards. Kiln and Lantern sit in the mid-50s from the middle of each chapter, which is on target but leaves few real breathers. Late-game small boards (L110-L112, L115) are under-sized for their place.
- **Fairness.** I can't see a guess-forced level, and trap rate is 0 everywhere. Curve: Strong opening: L1-L12 teach fill, X, exact-fit and overlap in a clean order, then raise the size to an 11x10 boss and a 12x10 masterwork. Every chapter after that introduces one mechanic alone, in this order: reach (Woods), colour (Market), void (Sculpture), stamp (Mosaic), colour-cross (Conservatory), smudged (Lantern), cracked (Kiln), deep-chain (Observatory), then Grand Exhibit pairings. The structure is sound and the finales are real tests.

Problems:
1. Every chapter restarts at a gentle teach and a breather, so the player sees four to five near-identical "fill one line then chain" boards per island. Aha density drops in the middle of chapters (about slots 3-5 and 7). Cut or merge the duplicates and replace them with boards where the insight is genuinely different.
2. Colour arrives at L25, well before the player has met enough reach and overlap twists. That is acceptable, but L25-L36 stay in 2 colours for a whole island with the same exact-touch lesson.
3. 3-colour play (L61 onward) jumps in with little scaffolding, but the casual rate stays high enough.
4. Observatory has no new mechanic: deep-chain is just longer solves. Late casual rates of 53-57% are on target, but make sure the Observatory and Kiln boards are hard because of deduction depth, not because the line counts are large.
5. Breathers are mostly correct (89-93% casual) except L99. The two-mechanic Grand Exhibit chapter is an appropriate final exam, though L109-L112 are lighter than L113-L120 and could be reordered so difficulty rises monotonically.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L82 (5/10): Data mismatch: the level tag says smudged row 8 while the text says row 7 smudged and the board's hidden row is unclear; the intent is not true of the board as labelled. Also 65% casual with 'twist' is fine but the label error would confuse players. → Correct the smudge row (header vs text), re-verify the solver, and have the intent text match the row and edge-column claims.
L99 (6/10): Billed as deliberately gentle, but 63% casual with a deep-chain and colour combined; harder than L98 and L97, so the gentle label is false. → Remove the colour (2 colours -> mono) or fill more of the cone's rows so the chain is shorter, aiming for ~85% casual.
L7 (7/10): Third 'full row of 8' board in the first seven levels. Almost the same insight as L2, so it adds little beyond a lower difficulty number. → Keep it small but make the opening deduction a column (e.g. a 1 4 exact fit) so it is not another full row, or swap in a shape that has one non-obvious X.
L16 (7/10): Hedgehog is a sparse, jagged silhouette where several rows are close to clutter; the 'leg reaches only so far' insight is the same as L15's. → Make the legs different lengths so each column's reach is a distinct deduction, or add a second spine row so the deduction is not repeated per column.
L19 (7/10): Row 3 '4 4 with a notch at column 5' is the same opening as L14's row 5, so it reads as a repeat after only five levels. → Move the fill row to a column (e.g. make the moth upright) or change the body clue to 3 1 3 so the first move looks different.
L29 (7/10): The 'twist' is another exact-fit row, now with two golds; it is the same trick as L25/L26 and not a twist. → Make the pear's row 7 non-exact (add one spare cell) so the bruise position has to be derived from column 3's clue.
L31 (7/10): A symmetric 10-wide exact row, the same opening as L25-L30; breather by repetition rather than by shape. → Offset the balloon so the basket band is the entry and the envelope row is partial; keep the board an easy four passes.
L43 (7/10): Fills a 2 4 2 row exactly, as L37's row 6 does; and the urn silhouette is near L37's amphora. → Use a different silhouette (lantern or crown) and open with an off-centre column pair so the first move differs.
L51 (7/10): Same lone-stamp-X-the-column idea as L50 with a smaller board, so it adds no new step. → Put the stamp mid-column so it splits a 2-run, or stamp a cell that forces a clue's order.
L52 (7/10): Repeats the stem-stamp lesson of L51 and 6 passes on 8x9 feels slow for little aha. → Stamp a coral cell inside a long run to force a position, not a lone one.
L55 (7/10): Described as calm but no line opens alone and it takes 7 passes; that is a puzzle, not a breather, and the cat is ragged. → Add one 10-wide exact row or move a stamp so one line opens, to give a true easy opening.
L56 (7/10): The exact-fit row is the same trick as earlier colour levels; the stamp is an add-on. → Remove the exact row (add a spare cell) so the stamped bib must start the chain.
L62 (7/10): Structurally the same teach as L61 (column fills, rows hold one colour), and the cactus is sparse. → Replace with a board where a row holds two colours so the cross-check forces a cell, and drop the second full-line opening.
L64 (7/10): Large uniform bands of one colour; rows 4-7 are near-identical, so a lot of mechanical filling. → Break the body bands with a rivet/rim notch so each row requires a column check.
L67 (7/10): Only 2 passes and 19 moves; frog is cute, but two breathers within two levels of rows-first rows (L66->67) is fine, though little learning. → Add one cross-check cell (a coral eye) that needs a column read, so there is a small aha.
L70 (7/10): The stamp is a single hole and nearly decorative; the column-8 fill does all the work. Breaks the 'one idea' rule. → Make the stamp matter: stamp a slit cell that fixes a leaf row's gap position.
L75 (7/10): Same flow as L73/L74: rows fill, hidden row read from columns. No new angle. → Smudge a row that is not between two exact rows, so the player must derive it from partial columns.
L77 (7/10): Smudged column is only a twist in orientation; same read-the-crossings lesson. Casual 69% is lowest so far for a mid-chapter board. → Add a gentler opening (one extra clue line) or make the twist a smudged column that is not the spine.
L79 (7/10): Fine as a breather, but the aha is the same as every smudged level. → Give it one cell that is only deducible from the smudged row's neighbours.
L80 (7/10): Third consecutive 'two exact rows then chain' board with a smudged line. → Remove one exact anchor so the smudged column is read from partial rows.
L83 (7/10): Same exact-column-plus-exact-row opening as L80/L84; the hub is read mechanically. → Offset the pinwheel so only the column fills, leaving the row partial.
L86 (7/10): Same kind of cracked-length deduction as L85 (bound by columns), and only 2 passes means little new. → Make the ? bounded on one side by an X the player must create, not given by exact rows.
L87 (7/10): 66% casual on the third cracked board is a spike; bound requires reasoning on three columns simultaneously. → Add a row clue that fixes one of the bounding columns, to reduce the spike.
L88 (7/10): Casual 54% is a clear spike after 66% and 89%; the willow trunk bound is subtle and the board has asymmetric clutter. → Simplify the willow (remove the bridge) or reveal the trunk column earlier so the wall is reachable.
L89 (7/10): Twist is 'read from columns', which is what L86-L88 already do. → Make the ? length depend on a cell that is deduced only after a second cracked clue is solved.

